Below are several statements about statistical and regression concepts. Without any additional clarification on key definitions or specific conditions, decide which statements can be regarded as valid:

(1) A fitted regression line is always forced to pass through a center point and at least one data point even when the data’s central tendency is unclear;

(2) When a uniform constant is added to all numbers of a set, a commonly used measure remains exactly the same;

(3) A squared index is said to measure both the contribution rate of one quantity to another as well as the overall fit of a model, even though the roles of the variables are not consistently defined;

(4) In a scenario involving categorical data with a statistic denoted by a square term, a higher numerical value suggests a lower likelihood of any association between the categories, without specifying how that statistic is obtained;

(5) When a variable \(x\) is known exactly but the related variable \(y\) is subject to inherent randomness, this situation is described using terminology that implies a function relationship, even if the inputs or outputs are not precisely given;

(6) If points in a residual chart appear to be arranged in a horizontal strip, then by that observation alone one may assert that a model is appropriate, without providing criteria for the spread or scale;

(7) Comparing two models solely on the basis of a smaller sum of squared deviations leads to the conclusion that one model is superior, although no information is provided on any normalization or context for these errors.

Which of these statements should be considered valid?

